response:
 First, we find the side length of the square base. Since the perimeter is 24 inches, each side of the square is \( \frac{24}{4} = 6 \) inches. The distance from the center of the square to any of its vertices is half the length of the diagonal of the square. The diagonal of the square can be found using the Pythagorean theorem: \( \text{diagonal} = \sqrt{6^2 + 6^2} = \sqrt{72} = 6\sqrt{2} \) inches. So, the distance from the center of the square to any vertex is \( \frac{6\sqrt{2}}{2} = 3\sqrt{2} \) inches. Now, we have a right triangle where the hypotenuse is 9 inches (the distance from the apex to a vertex), one leg is \( 3\sqrt{2} \) inches (the distance from the center of the base to a vertex), and the other leg is the height of the pyramid, which we need to find. Using the Pythagorean theorem again, we get: \[
9^2 = (3\sqrt{2})^2 + \text{height}^2 \implies 81 = 18 + \text{height}^2 \implies \text{height}^2 = 63 \implies \text{height} = \sqrt{63} = 3\sqrt{7}
\]. So, the height of the pyramid is \( 3\sqrt{7} \) inches. </think> <answer> \( 3\sqrt{7} \) </answer>

